Fillets: a blend rolling on an edge that comes to an edge turning towards
the ball touches that edge before the vertex and rolls on it from there,
with a piece of the ball between the two blends (mitcad#237, with 0170).

Where a rounding overflows its face onto the edge beyond (0170), the edge
E1 it rolls on can end at a vertex V where the next edge E2 of the
reference face (the face the ball overflows) turns towards the ball (a
rib's far side that thickens towards the near side's offset, a thin rim
whose inner edge turns into a spoke). The ball then touches E2 before it
comes to V: with both contacts on the restriction the walk went on rolling
on E1 into the solid beyond E2, up to V, and the next blend started on E2
at V, past the ball that touches both (on a rib, radius 3: 2.6e-3 off).

- Blend_SurfRstFunction::NextCurveAtEnd (default none), given by
  BRepBlend_SurfRstConstRad::SetNextCurves: the curves of the next edges at
  the restriction's ends, as ChFi3d_NextEdges finds them.
- BRepBlend_SurfRstLineBuilder: where a section's ball reaches into such a
  next edge at a point inside it (NextEdgeGap: its distance from the
  centre less the radius), the walk finds where it touches it, between the
  last section clear of it and this one (FindConcaveTurn: regula falsi on
  the guide's parameter), and ends there on the restriction, inside it.
- ChFi3d_Builder::StartSol: a side rolling on an edge that ended inside it
  where its ball touches the next edge of the reference face at a vertex of
  the edge (ChFi3d_ConcaveTurnEdge) goes on rolling on that edge, from the
  ball's contact on it; PerformSetOfSurfOnElSpine starts the next blend
  with that same ball, at the guide's parameter of its contacts' mid-point.
- Between the two blends the rounding is the ball itself
  (ChFi3d_ConcaveTurnWedge): A's last section and B's first are arcs of
  the ball from its contact on the other face (q, shared) to E1 and to E2.
  The pieces of its sphere between them, its pole at q, are SurfDatas whose
  side on the other face is the point q (a degenerated edge, as 0171) and
  whose other side runs where the sphere cuts the faces beyond E1 and E2,
  across their common edge (one piece per face). E1 and E2 end at the
  blends' contacts on both sides (the transitions EXTERNAL): before them
  the blends' contacts take their place, beyond them the ball's piece
  removes them. Where those pieces cannot be made (the edges' contacts
  more than a right angle round the ball from q, so that the sphere cuts
  neither face between the sections) the stripe fails.

Upstream: the change is local to the walk on a restriction and the
stripe's chaining; without an edge turning towards the ball nothing
changes.
